{"name":"LED Blue","hex":"#0066a3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"led","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Lendu."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["simple past and past participle of lead"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/led"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Light-emitting diode","blocks":[{"text":"A light-emitting diode (LED) is an electronic component that uses a semiconductor to emit light when current flows through it. Electrons in the semiconductor recombine with electron holes, thereby releasing energy in the form of photons. The color of the light is determined by the energy required for electrons to cross the band gap of the semiconductor. White light is obtained by using multiple semiconductors or a layer of light-emitting phosphor on the semiconductor device."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cb/RBG-LED.jpg/330px-RBG-LED.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Light-emitting_diode","wikidata":"Q25504"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Surface_mount_LED_close_up_image.png/500px-Surface_mount_LED_close_up_image.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Surface_mount_LED_close_up_image.png/1280px-Surface_mount_LED_close_up_image.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Surface_mount_LED_close_up_image.png/500px-Surface_mount_LED_close_up_image.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Close-up image of a surface-mount LED","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASurface_mount_LED_close_up_image.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Br20_1.jpg/500px-Br20_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Br20_1.jpg/500px-Br20_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A bulb-shaped retrofit LED lamp with aluminum heat sink, a light diffusing dome and E27 screw base, using a built-in power supply working on mains voltage","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABr20_1.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Blue_light_emitting_diodes_over_a_proto-board.jpg/500px-Blue_light_emitting_diodes_over_a_proto-board.jpg?utm_sourc